@gabikliot I can help to fix this, I just need to better understand the fix.
Currently `DeserializationContext` is internal, should we make it public?
We will then change the generated deserialization code to something like this:

``` C#
public static global::System.Object Deserializer(global::System.Type expected, global::Orleans.Serialization.BinaryTokenStreamReader stream)
{
    MyObserverSubscriptionManager<T> result = new MyObserverSubscriptionManager<T>();
// This bit is new
    DeserializationContext.Current.RecordObject(stream.CurrentPosition, result);
    result.@Foo = (int)SerializationManager.@DeserializeInner(typeof (int), stream);
    field1.@SetValue(result, (HashSet<T>)SerializationManager.@DeserializeInner(typeof (HashSet<T>), stream));
    return result;
}
```

In `SerializationManager.DeserializeInner` we will remove the last call to `RecordObject`, the call from this part:

``` C#
var deser = GetDeserializer(resultType);
if (deser != null)
{
    result = deser(resultType, stream);
    DeserializationContext.Current.RecordObject(start, result);
    return result;
}
```

Does that sound right?
I implemented this change, but it fails for the built-in deserializers. It seems cumbersome to follow all non-generated calls to `DeserializeInner` with `RecordObject`. It is benign to leave the call to `RecordObject` in `DeserializeInner`, so I am rerunning tests with that in place.
Your first comment above looks correct. That is more or less what I meant. Of course, compiled with eyes, so can't be 100% sure.

Not sure about your later comment on built in deserializers. You don't want to store the same object twice in the deser context.
